      "textEn": "Why shouldn't it be received? References: 1.Sri Chaitanya Charitamrita (9.41) The essence of this statement is found in Sri Chaitanya Charitamrita (9.41). God\nChaitanya has said—\n\nIn the land of India, this human birth is a friend. Birth meaningful, do good to others. Every creature born in the land of India, especially man, can achieve ultimate success through Vedic knowledge and its practice. Upon being self-evident, he can serve the entire human society for self-\nrealization. This is the best way to do good. In memory, there is suffering, then the change of fruit. Abhayam chaapyanihaayaam sankalpadvirametkaviha. 59. Meaning of the word\n\nSmritva—by remembering; Ehaayaam—in the field of action done with the intention of the fruits; Pariklesham—the loss of power and\nthe pathetic situation; Tatah—from that; Phala-viparyayam—the opposite of the desired fruit; Abhayam—fearlessness; Cha—also; Api—indeed; Nisanshayam—without doubt;\nAnihaayaam—when there is no desire for the fruits of action; Sankalpat—from material desires; Viramet—to stop;\nShould be; Kavih—the wise man.\nKeeping in mind that great obstacles come in the field of accomplished actions for the sake of the fruits of action,\nand opposite results are obtained by will, whether they arise from physical actions or are the fruits of actions described by Vedic texts,\na wise man should renounce the desire for actions\nbecause such efforts do not lead to achieving the ultimate purpose of life. On the other hand, if someone performs actions with a desireless attitude for the sake of the fruits of their actions, that is, if they\nare engaged in devotional activities, then, free from miserable situations, they can attain the ultimate purpose of life. Considering this, man should renounce physical desires."
