      "textEn": "Thus, under the sixth canto of Srimad Bhagavat, \"The Meeting of King Chitraketu with the Supreme Being\"\nThe sixteen chapters named Bhakti Vedanta Tatparya are completed. Chapter Seventeen. \n\nThe curse by Mata Parvati to Chitraketu. \n\nIn this chapter, there is a description of Chitraketu attaining a demonic body due to laughing with Lord Shiva. After talking to the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Chitraketu, along with the women of the Vidyadhar Loka,\nstarted enjoying pleasures in his plane. After performing a collective kirtan of God's glory, he\ntook off in his plane into outer space. One day, while flying like this, he reached the groves of Sumeru mountain.\nWhere Shivji and Parvati, surrounded by Siddhas, Charanas, and Sadhus, were embracing. Seeing Shivji in that state, Chitraketu laughed loudly, which\nParvati became extremely angry and cursed him. From this curse, Chitraketu later took birth as an Asura named Vritrasura.\nBut Chitraketu was not at all afraid of Parvati's curse. He spoke thus—\"Every creature\nAccording to its past deeds, enjoys happiness and sorrow in human society and wanders in this material\nworld. Therefore, he is responsible for his own happiness and sorrow. In this world,\nevery creature is controlled by physical nature, yet it considers itself the doer of every object. In this world, the man who is born from the external power of God is also cursed.\nHe sometimes attains a boon, and in this way, he sometimes enjoys the pleasures of heaven, but\nsometimes suffers the sorrows of the underworld. But all these situations are the same because they are in this material world. None of these situations have a permanent existence, because all of them are temporary. Bhagwan hi"
