      "textEn": "The great yogis, sages, and accomplished charanas are praised. 2. In the fountains of the Kulachalendra, in the fulfillment of the Nanasakalp. Reme Vidyadhareerishabhirgapayanhariishwaram. 3. Meaning of the word:\n\nSaha—He (Chitraketu); Laksha—One lakh; Varsha—Year; Lakshaanam— Of one lakh; Avyahata— Uninterrupted; Bal-\nIndriya—Of whose senses, strength and power; Stuyamanah— Being praised; Maha-Yogi— Supreme Yogi; Munibhishah— By the sages;\nSiddha-Charanaih— By the Siddhas and Charanas; Kulachalendra-Dronishu— In the valleys of Mount Kulachala or Sumeru;\nNana-Sankalpa-Siddhishu— Where all kinds of yoga powers are attained; Reme— Having enjoyed; Vidyadhar-Striibhih— With the women of Vidyadhar Loka; Gapayan— Praising; Harim— Lord, Hari; Ishvaram;—the controller. Praised by great saints and sages and the inhabitants of Siddhaloka and Charnaloka,\nThe most powerful Yogi Chitraketu, enjoying the bliss of life for millions of years,\nremained wandering.\nWithout the physical strength and senses diminishing, he kept wandering in the valleys of the Sumeru mountain.\nThere, which is the place of the attainment of various types of yogic powers. He\nWhile chanting the glory of Lord Hari, he enjoyed life with the women of the Vidyadharaloka.\nHe enjoyed. Meaning: It is worth noting that although Maharaj Chitraketu was surrounded by the unique\nbeautiful women of the Vidyadharaloka, he did not forget to chant the holy name of God and sing their praises. It has been proven at many places that one who is not tainted by any physical state,\nand who is a pure devotee who chants the glory of God, such a pure devotee should be considered accomplished.\nShould be. Once upon a time, there was a plane, Vishnudatta, who was bright. The mountain is seen, the surroundings are seen, the accomplished ones are seen. 4>. Aalingyankritam devim bahunam munisansadi.\nThus spoke the goddess, and the goddess, hearing this, was greatly agitated. 5. Meaning of the word\n\nOnce—once; Sah—He (King Chitraketu); Vimanena—by airplane; Vishnu-Dattena—by Lord Vishnu;\nBhaswata—luminous, radiant; Girisham—to Lord Shiva; Dadrase—seen; Gachchant—going; Paritam—surrounded;"
